When the parser detects a `<div>` element, it does one of two things depending on the class name:

### 1. Highlighted Code Wrapper (`class="highlight"`)
Many blogs and CMS platforms wrap code elements inside a syntax-highlighting wrapper like `<div class="highlight"><pre><code>...</code></pre></div>`.
* **Action**: The parser detects this specific class, extracts the programming language (e.g., `"rust"`, `"python"`), extracts the raw code text, and pushes it as a **`ContentBlock::CodeBlock`**.

### 2. Generic Container (`<div>` with any other class or no class)
If it is a normal layout div:
* **Action**: The parser **unwraps it** and recurses into its children (`blocks.extend(parse_block_nodes(node.children()))`). 
* This ensures that any structural blocks nested inside layout grids or wrapper divs (like paragraphs, tables, lists, or headings) are parsed and collected.

---

Here is the code handling this in [`html_2_json/src/parser.rs`](file:///Users/abhishekhprajapati/Projects/rust-rn-inte-prep/html_2_json/src/parser.rs#L427-L468):
```rust
"div" => {
    let classes = element.attr("class").unwrap_or("");
    let is_highlight = classes.split_whitespace().any(|c| c == "highlight");

    if is_highlight {
        // Detects the nested <pre><code> and parses as a CodeBlock...
    } else {
        // Generic div — unwrap and parse children
        blocks.extend(parse_block_nodes(node.children()));
    }
}
```

The resulting JSON depends on which type of `<div>` is parsed:

### Case 1: Highlighted Code Wrapper (`class="highlight"`)
For HTML like this:
```html
<div class="highlight">
  <pre><code class="language-js">console.log("hello");</code></pre>
</div>
```
The result is a **`CodeBlock`** object:
```json
[
  {
    "CodeBlock": {
      "language": "js",
      "code": "console.log(\"hello\");"
    }
  }
]
```

---

### Case 2: Generic Layout Container (`<div>`)
For HTML like this:
```html
<div class="news-container">
  <p>First Paragraph</p>
  <p>Second Paragraph</p>
</div>
```
The `<div>` is unwrapped and ignored. The nested structural blocks are parsed directly into the parent array:
```json
[
  {
    "Paragraph": {
      "children": [
        { "Text": { "text": "First Paragraph" } }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "Paragraph": {
      "children": [
        { "Text": { "text": "Second Paragraph" } }
      ]
    }
  }
]
```
*(There is no `"Div"` or `"Container"` block in the JSON, preventing unnecessary layout nesting for the React Native developer).*

If you have nested `<div>`s wrapping paragraphs like this:

```html
<div>
  <p>First paragraph</p>
  <div>
    <p>Second paragraph</p>
  </div>
</div>
```

The resulting JSON will be a **flat list of the two paragraphs in their exact order**:

```json
[
  {
    "Paragraph": {
      "children": [
        {
          "Text": {
            "text": "First paragraph"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  {
    "Paragraph": {
      "children": [
        {
          "Text": {
            "text": "Second paragraph"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  }
]
```

### Why it behaves this way:
1. The parser hits the outer `<div>` and recurses into its children, immediately parsing `<p>First paragraph</p>` as a `Paragraph` block.
2. Next, it hits the inner `<div>`. Since it has no special class like `highlight`, the parser unwraps it and recurses again, parsing `<p>Second paragraph</p>` as the next `Paragraph` block.
3. All parsed blocks are collected into the same flat list, keeping the JSON structure clean and flat for rendering.

