# JSI Zero-Copy Integration Roadmap

This document outlines the step-by-step roadmap to cross-compile the `html_2_json` Rust library and integrate it into a React Native library using **JSI (JavaScript Interface) HostObjects** for zero-copy memory access.

---

## 🗺️ Roadmap Overview

```mermaid
graph TD
  A[1. Rust Cross-Compilation] --> B[2. Define C FFI Boundary]
  B --> C[3. Create RN JSI Library Template]
  C --> D[4. Write C++ JSI HostObjects]
  D --> E[5. Link Native Binaries & Build]
```

---

## 🛠️ Step 1: Rust Cross-Compilation Setup

We configure Cargo to compile both a static library (`.a`) for iOS and a shared library (`.so`) for Android.

### 1.1 Configure `Cargo.toml`
Update your library's `html_2_json/Cargo.toml` to specify the output crate types:
```toml
[lib]
name = "html_2_json"
crate-type = ["staticlib", "cdylib"]  # staticlib is for iOS, cdylib is for Android
```

### 1.2 Add Compilation Targets
Install the native compiler targets on your build machine:
```bash
# iOS Targets
rustup target add aarch64-apple-ios x86_64-apple-ios aarch64-apple-ios-sim

# Android Targets
rustup target add aarch64-linux-android armv7-linux-androideabi i686-linux-android x86_64-linux-android
```

### 1.3 Compile the Binaries
* **For iOS**:
  ```bash
  cargo build --target aarch64-apple-ios --release
  cargo build --target aarch64-apple-ios-sim --release
  ```
* **For Android** (Requires Android NDK configured on your system):
  We use `cargo-ndk` to simplify cross-compiling with Android’s clang toolchain:
  ```bash
  cargo install cargo-ndk
  cargo ndk -t arm64-v8a -o ./jniLibs build --release
  ```

---

## 🔗 Step 2: Define the C FFI Boundary in Rust

Since JSI is C++, we must expose a flat C FFI from our Rust codebase. We create a module that returns opaque pointers and exposes raw getters so C++ can query properties on-demand.

Create a new file `html_2_json/src/ffi.rs`:
```rust
use std::ffi::{CString, CStr};
use std::os::raw::c_char;
use crate::models::ContentBlock;
use crate::parse_html;

// Opaque container to pass across the FFI boundary
pub struct ParsedArticle {
    pub blocks: Vec<ContentBlock>,
}

#[no_mangle]
pub extern "C" fn parse_html_ffi(html_ptr: *const c_char) -> *mut ParsedArticle {
    if html_ptr.is_null() { return std::ptr::null_mut(); }

    let c_str = unsafe { CStr::from_ptr(html_ptr) };
    let html = c_str.to_string_lossy();

    let blocks = parse_html(&html);
    Box::into_raw(Box::new(ParsedArticle { blocks }))
}

#[no_mangle]
pub extern "C" fn free_article_ffi(article: *mut ParsedArticle) {
    if !article.is_null() {
        unsafe { let _ = Box::from_raw(article); }
    }
}

// Getters for Lazy Evaluation inside JSI HostObjects
#[no_mangle]
pub extern "C" fn get_block_count(article: *const ParsedArticle) -> usize {
    if article.is_null() { return 0; }
    unsafe { (*article).blocks.len() }
}

#[no_mangle]
pub extern "C" fn get_block_type(article: *const ParsedArticle, index: usize) -> *mut c_char {
    if article.is_null() { return std::ptr::null_mut(); }
    let blocks = unsafe { &(*article).blocks };
    if index >= blocks.len() { return std::ptr::null_mut(); }

    let type_str = match &blocks[index] {
        ContentBlock::Paragraph { .. } => "Paragraph",
        ContentBlock::Heading { .. } => "Heading",
        ContentBlock::Separator { .. } => "Separator",
        ContentBlock::Table { .. } => "Table",
        ContentBlock::CodeBlock { .. } => "CodeBlock",
        _ => "Unknown",
    };

    CString::new(type_str).unwrap().into_raw()
}

#[no_mangle]
pub extern "C" fn free_string_ffi(s: *mut c_char) {
    if !s.is_null() {
        unsafe { let _ = CString::from_raw(s); }
    }
}
```

---

## 📦 Step 3: Create the React Native Library Template

1. Initialize a new React Native native library:
   ```bash
   npx react-native-builder-bob@latest create react-native-html-parser
   ```
   Select **C++ / JSI Module** when prompted.

2. **Directory Structure**:
   * Put Rust headers into `cpp/`.
   * Bundle your compiled iOS `.a` static libraries inside `ios/libs/`.
   * Bundle your Android `.so` libraries in `android/src/main/jniLibs/`.

---

## ⚡ Step 4: Write the C++ JSI HostObjects

Inside `cpp/HTMLParserHostObjects.h`, we declare C++ `jsi::HostObject`s that wrap our Rust struct pointers.

```cpp
#pragma once
#include <jsi/jsi.h>

extern "C" {
    // Declarations matching the Rust FFI
    struct ParsedArticle;
    ParsedArticle* parse_html_ffi(const char* html);
    void free_article_ffi(ParsedArticle* article);
    size_t get_block_count(const ParsedArticle* article);
    char* get_block_type(const ParsedArticle* article, size_t index);
    void free_string_ffi(char* s);
}

using namespace facebook;

// HostObject representing the entire parsed article
class ArticleHostObject : public jsi::HostObject {
private:
    ParsedArticle* m_article;

public:
    ArticleHostObject(const char* html) {
        m_article = parse_html_ffi(html);
    }

    ~ArticleHostObject() {
        free_article_ffi(m_article);
    }

    // JS gets lazy access via properties (e.g. article.length or article.getBlock(i))
    jsi::Value get(jsi::Runtime& rt, const jsi::PropNameID& name) override {
        auto propName = name.utf8(rt);

        if (propName == "length") {
            return jsi::Value((int)get_block_count(m_article));
        }

        if (propName == "getBlock") {
            return jsi::Function::createFromHostFunction(
                rt, name, 1,
                [this](jsi::Runtime& rt, const jsi::Value& thisVal, const jsi::Value* args, size_t count) -> jsi::Value {
                    int index = args[0].asNumber();
                    char* raw_type = get_block_type(m_article, index);

                    // Construct a child HostObject representing this block
                    jsi::Object blockObj = jsi::Object(rt);
                    blockObj.setProperty(rt, "type", jsi::String::createFromUtf8(rt, raw_type));

                    free_string_ffi(raw_type);
                    return blockObj; // Return on-demand object
                }
            );
        }

        return jsi::Value::undefined();
    }
};
```

---

## 🚀 Step 5: Initialize the JSI Module in JS

Register the global JSI function on startup inside `cpp/html-parser.cpp`:

```cpp
#include "HTMLParserHostObjects.h"

void installHTMLParser(jsi::Runtime& jsiRuntime) {
    auto parseHTML = jsi::Function::createFromHostFunction(
        jsiRuntime,
        jsi::PropNameID::forAscii(jsiRuntime, "parseHTML"),
        1,
        [](jsi::Runtime& rt, const jsi::Value& thisVal, const jsi::Value* args, size_t count) -> jsi::Value {
            std::string html = args[0].asString(rt).utf8(rt);

            // Return the HostObject representing the parsed native memory
            return jsi::Object::createFromHostObject(rt, std::make_shared<ArticleHostObject>(html.c_str()));
        }
    );

    jsiRuntime.global().setProperty(jsiRuntime, "parseHTML", std::move(parseHTML));
}
```

Now, in your React Native JavaScript/TypeScript app:
```typescript
// Call synchronous native parser (zero-copy JSI host object)
const article = global.parseHTML("<h1>Hello World</h1><p>...</p>");

console.log(article.length); // Accesses Rust memory dynamically!
const firstBlock = article.getBlock(0); // Instantiates only the first block object on the JS heap
console.log(firstBlock.type); // "Heading"
```

